.include {docs}

# go2_calibration

`go2_calibration` measures how the lidar and the IMU are mounted on the Unitree Go2. It writes the
result into a ROS 2 parameter file, and `go2_preprocessor` reads that file.

**What.** One program (`ros2 run go2_calibration go2_calibration`). The robot stands still, spins
and tilts for about 97 s while the program records its sensors. Then it computes a few numbers:

- where the lidar sits on the body, and how it is rotated;
- the bias and the scale error of the body IMU;
- how much too long the lidar measures every distance.

**Why.** Point-LIO builds the map from the lidar cloud and the IMU. Both must be in the same frame,
the body frame, and their numbers must be right. A wrong rotation bends the whole map.

Nothing is typed into the code. CMU's `calibrate_imu` keeps the lidar rotation of its own robot in
the code. On our firmware that rotation is wrong by about 25 deg, and their method cannot see it.
Here every rotation is measured, and every result is checked. If one check fails, **no file is
written**, so a bad calibration cannot reach the map by accident.

**When.** Once per robot. Again after a firmware update, after the lidar was remounted, or after a
hard fall.

## Where it sits

.mermaid
    flowchart TB
      subgraph robot["Go2 topics"]
        cloud["/utlidar/cloud"]
        base["/utlidar/cloud_base"]
        limu["/utlidar/imu"]
        state["/sportmodestate"]
      end
      robot --> cal[go2_calibration]
      cal -->|"/api/sport/request"| robot
      cal -->|"/calibration/phase"| bag[(bag)]
      cal --> yaml[(go2_calibration.yaml)]
      bag -.->|"bag:=..."| cal
      yaml --> pre[go2_preprocessor]
      pre --> lio[Point-LIO]
      script["tools/mapping/calibration.sh"] -.->|runs and records| cal

`tools/mapping/calibration.sh` is the easy way to run it. It records the bag, runs the program, and
points `~/calibration/current.yaml` at the new file. `map.sh` then uses that link.

## What it measures

| Result | What it means | Taken from | Written to the file |
|---|---|---|---|
| `lidar_to_body` | Rotation and position of the lidar on the body | `/utlidar/cloud` against its body-frame copy `/utlidar/cloud_base`, robot still | yes |
| Floor tilt | How tilted the floor looks after `lidar_to_body`. This checks `lidar_to_body` by other means | The floor in the raw cloud | no, check only |
| `range_offset` | The lidar measures every distance this much too long | How the flat floor bends into a bowl | yes |
| `body_imu.gyro_bias` | What the body gyroscope shows when still | `/sportmodestate`, robot still | yes |
| `body_imu.accel_scale` | `9.81` divided by what the still body accelerometer reads | `/sportmodestate`, robot still | yes |
| Body gravity direction | Is the still accelerometer pointing up? | `/sportmodestate`, robot still | no, check only |
| `imu_to_body` | Rotation from the lidar IMU to the body | Both gyroscopes while the robot turns and tilts | no, report only |
| Lidar IMU `gyro_bias`, `gyro_leak`, gravity | Same kind of numbers for the lidar IMU | Lidar IMU | no, report only |

`go2_preprocessor` gives Point-LIO the body IMU only. The lidar IMU results stay as comments in the
file, but their checks still count: one failure stops the file. The angle between `imu_to_body` and
`lidar_to_body` tells whether the body IMU axes agree with the lidar mount (0.65 deg on our Go2).

## Quick start

On the robot, on a flat floor with 1 m free around it:

```bash
tools/mapping/calibration.sh
```

By hand:

```bash
source ~/unitree_ros2/setup.sh
source ~/unitree_go2/install/setup.bash
ros2 run go2_calibration go2_calibration
```

Without moving anything, from a recorded bag (it must contain `/calibration/phase`):

```bash
ros2 run go2_calibration go2_calibration --ros-args -p bag:=<bag folder> -p output:=<file>
```

Use the result with `go2_preprocessor --ros-args --params-file go2_calibration.yaml`.

## How it works

### The code in one picture

The program is a straight line: **read settings, fill a buffer, calibrate, report, write the file**.
Two sources can fill the buffer, the live robot or a bag. Both make the same calls, so
`calibrate()` never knows where the data came from.

An arrow means "uses".

.mermaid
    flowchart TB
      subgraph ROS["go2_calibration_ros: needs ROS"]
        main["main: settings, flow, Ctrl-C"]
        live["live_source: run_live"]
        bag["bag_source: read_bag"]
        msg["messages: topics, conversions"]
      end
      subgraph CORE["go2_calibration_core: no ROS"]
        seq["sequence: phases and commands"]
        buf["buffer: CalibrationBuffer"]
        cal["calibrate: the calibration and checks"]
        fit["fitting: Kabsch fits"]
        flo["floor: RANSAC plane, range offset"]
        imu["imu_fit: pairing, lag, leak, stats"]
        rep["report: text and yaml"]
      end
      main --> live
      main --> bag
      main --> cal
      main --> rep
      main --> seq
      live --> msg
      bag --> msg
      live --> seq
      live --> buf
      bag --> buf
      msg --> buf
      msg --> seq
      buf --> seq
      buf --> imu
      cal --> buf
      cal --> fit
      cal --> flo
      cal --> imu
      imu --> fit
      rep --> cal

Everything in the `core` box is plain C++ with Eigen (a matrix library). It has no ROS and is unit
tested. The `ros` box is thin: it only reads and writes messages.

### A live run, step by step

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
      actor Op as Operator
      participant M as main
      participant L as run_live
      participant R as Go2 robot
      participant B as CalibrationBuffer
      participant C as calibrate
      participant P as report
      Op->>M: ros2 run go2_calibration
      M->>M: read_settings, build_sequence
      M->>Op: warning and 5 s countdown
      M->>L: run_live(phases, buffer)
      loop up to 5 s
        R-->>L: cloud, cloud_base, imu, sportmodestate
      end
      loop 10 phases, 50 Hz
        L->>R: Move, Euler or StopMove
        L->>B: set_phase(name)
        R-->>L: sensor messages
        L->>B: add_lidar_imu, add_body_imu, add_scan
      end
      L->>R: StopMove for 0.5 s
      L-->>M: completed
      M->>C: calibrate(buffer.data(), limits)
      C-->>M: Calibration with 11 checks
      M->>P: describe, to_parameter_file
      P-->>M: report lines and yaml text
      M->>Op: report, and the file if all checks PASS

`main.cpp` in four steps:

1. **Settings.** Every setting is a ROS parameter with a default. `build_sequence` rejects a value
   out of range **before anything moves**.
2. **Fill the buffer.** Live: warn, count down 5 s (Ctrl-C cancels, nothing moves), `run_live`. Bag:
   `read_bag`.
3. **Calibrate and print.** `calibrate()` throws a clear error if data is missing (no floor, no
   turning, no still sample).
4. **Write.** Only if every check passed. The exit code is 0 only then.

### The motion

The live source runs ten phases. A phase has a name, a length and one sport command.

.mermaid
    flowchart TB
      subgraph turns["Turns"]
        direction LR
        s1["still 30 s"] --> s2["spin_left 12.6 s"]
        s2 --> s3["pause 3 s"]
        s3 --> s4["spin_right 12.6 s"]
        s4 --> s5["pause 3 s"]
      end
      subgraph tilts["Tilts"]
        direction LR
        s6["balance_stand 1 s"] --> s7["tilt_pitch 15 s"]
        s7 --> s8["tilt_roll 15 s"]
        s8 --> s9["level 2 s"]
        s9 --> s10["pause 3 s"]
      end
      s5 --> s6

| Phase | Command | Sample use | Why |
|---|---|---|---|
| `still` | StopMove | **still** | Bias, lidar mount, floor, range offset need a robot that does not move |
| `spin_left`, `spin_right` | Move(0, 0, +1 or -1 rad/s), 2 turns each | **moving** | The two IMUs must see the same rotation |
| `tilt_pitch`, `tilt_roll` | Euler, 0.2 rad sine at 0.4 Hz | **moving** | Turns about the other two axes. Turns alone leave those axes unknown |
| `pause`, `balance_stand`, `level` | StopMove, BalanceStand (sent once), Euler(0, 0, 0) | none | Transitions. Their samples are dropped |

The length of a spin is `turns * 2 * pi / rate` = 2 * 6.283 / 1.0 = 12.57 s. All phases together:
30 + 12.57 + 3 + 12.57 + 3 + 1 + 15 + 15 + 2 + 3 = **97.1 s**. With `motion.tilt_angle:=0` the tilts
are left out and the run is 61.1 s.

The phase name is also the **label** of the samples. `use_of(name)` is the only place that decides:
`still` is still, names starting with `spin_` or `tilt_` are moving, anything else is dropped. The
live source publishes the name on `/calibration/phase` at 50 Hz, so a bag recorded alongside can be
replayed later.

### The live source and its safety

`run_live` has three parts: subscribe, wait for the sensors, run the phases.

.mermaid
    flowchart TD
      start(["run_live"]) --> wait{"all 4 sensor topics seen?"}
      wait -->|"no, 5 s passed"| nodata(["no_data: nothing moved"])
      wait -->|"no, Ctrl-C"| stopped0(["stopped: nothing moved"])
      wait -->|yes| tick["spin_some: read messages"]
      tick --> ctrlc{"stop requested?"}
      ctrlc -->|yes| stop["stop_robot: StopMove for 0.5 s"]
      ctrlc -->|no| silent{"/sportmodestate silent for 0.5 s?"}
      silent -->|yes| stop
      silent -->|no| sp{"setpoint_at(elapsed)"}
      sp -->|"none: sequence over"| stop
      sp -->|"command due, every 20 ms"| send["send command, publish phase, buffer.set_phase"]
      send --> tick
      sp -->|"not due yet"| tick
      stop --> result(["completed, stopped or robot_silent"])

Safety rules, all in this loop:

- Nothing moves until all four sensor topics have sent a message.
- **Every** way out sends StopMove for 0.5 s: the end, Ctrl-C, SIGTERM, a silent robot and any
  exception. `main` handles Ctrl-C itself, because rclcpp's own handler would shut ROS down before the
  robot got its StopMove.
- Speed and angle have hard limits (1.5 rad/s, 0.3 rad, 1 Hz). No parameter can go above them.
- Commands go out at 50 Hz, like Unitree's examples. The loop itself spins at 1000 Hz. At 50 Hz it
  kept only 1 IMU sample in 5, and each was old.
- Samples get their **receive time**: it is the only clock both IMUs share. A bag stores the same
  receive time, so live and bag give the same data.

.mermaid
    stateDiagram-v2
      [*] --> Waiting
      Waiting --> Running: 4 topics alive
      Waiting --> [*]: 5 s without data, or Ctrl-C
      Running --> Stopping: end, Ctrl-C, SIGTERM, 0.5 s silence, error
      Stopping --> [*]: 0.5 s of StopMove sent

### The bag source

`read_bag` replays a recorded bag, message by message, into the buffer. It makes the same calls
as the live source. It opens the bag, checks that all five topics are in it (the four sensors
plus `/calibration/phase`), then:

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
      participant F as bag file
      participant S as read_bag
      participant B as CalibrationBuffer
      loop every message, in recording order
        F-->>S: topic, receive time, bytes
        alt /calibration/phase
          S->>B: set_phase(name)
        else /utlidar/imu
          S->>B: add_lidar_imu(time, gyro, accel)
        else /sportmodestate
          S->>B: add_body_imu(time, gyro, accel)
        else a cloud and the buffer wants scans
          S->>B: add_scan(stamp, points, is_body_copy)
        end
      end

A cloud is only decoded if `wants_scans()` is true. Decoding is the slow part.

### The buffer

`CalibrationBuffer` sorts every sample into a box, depending on the **current phase**.

.mermaid
    flowchart LR
      ph["set_phase(name)"] --> use{"use_of(name)"}
      use -->|still| st["still_gyro, still_accel, still_body_gyro, still_body_accel, scans"]
      use -->|moving| mv["moving_lidar_gyro, moving_body_gyro"]
      use -->|none| drop((dropped))
      li["add_lidar_imu"] --> use
      bi["add_body_imu"] --> use
      sc["add_scan"] --> use

`set_phase` only counts a change of name, because the live source repeats it 50 times a second.

**Matching the two clouds.** The firmware publishes every scan twice: `/utlidar/cloud` in the lidar
frame and `/utlidar/cloud_base` in the body frame. Both carry the same stamp. The copy that arrives
first waits in a map. When the second arrives, the points are paired by a **key** of
`(time, intensity, ring)`:

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
      participant S as source
      participant B as CalibrationBuffer
      S->>B: add_scan(stamp 100, lidar frame)
      Note over B: no partner yet, keep it in waiting_lidar_
      S->>B: add_scan(stamp 100, body frame)
      Note over B: partner found, match() by key
      B->>B: lidar_points and body_points get the pairs
      B->>B: floor_points get every lidar point
      B->>B: scans + 1

Example, made-up numbers: the point with key `(t=0.0123, intensity=40, ring=3)` is at
`(1.0, 0.2, -0.3)` in the lidar scan and at `(-0.3, 1.0, -0.2)` in the body scan. They become one
pair. A key that appears twice in one scan is skipped, because it cannot be told apart. The buffer
keeps at most `max_scans` (100) matched scans, only while the robot is still. A phase change throws
away scans still waiting for a partner.

### The calibration

`calibrate(data, limits)` is a pure function: numbers in, numbers out. It has six steps.

.mermaid
    flowchart LR
      subgraph inputs["CalibrationData"]
        lp["lidar_points, body_points"]
        fp["floor_points"]
        sbg["still_body_gyro"]
        sba["still_body_accel"]
        mlg["moving_lidar_gyro"]
        mbg["moving_body_gyro"]
        slg["still_gyro, still_accel"]
      end
      lp -->|"1 fit_rigid"| l2b["lidar_to_body"]
      fp -->|"1 find_floor"| plane["floor plane"]
      plane --> tilt["floor tilt"]
      l2b --> tilt
      fp -->|"1 fit_range_offset"| ro["range_offset"]
      plane --> ro
      sbg -->|"2 mean_and_std"| gb["body gyro_bias"]
      sba -->|"2 mean_and_std"| asc["accel_scale, body tilt"]
      mlg -->|"3 fit_imu_rotation"| i2b["imu_to_body and lag"]
      mbg --> i2b
      mlg -->|"3 axis_spread"| spread["axis spread"]
      i2b --> ang["angle to lidar_to_body"]
      l2b --> ang
      slg -->|"4 rotate and mean"| lgb["lidar gyro_bias, gravity tilt"]
      i2b --> lgb
      mlg -->|"5 fit_leak"| leak["gyro_leak"]
      lgb --> leak
      i2b --> leak
      gb --> chk["6 eleven checks"]
      asc --> chk
      l2b --> chk
      tilt --> chk
      i2b --> chk
      spread --> chk
      lgb --> chk
      leak --> chk

#### 1. Lidar mount and floor

*Lidar mount.* The paired points are the same physical points in two frames. The best rotation `R`
and shift `t` that carry the first set onto the second is found with the **Kabsch method**:

1. subtract the centre of each point set;
2. add up `from * to^T` for all pairs, a 3x3 table;
3. its SVD (a standard matrix split, `U S V^T`) gives the rotation `R = V U^T`. If that turns out
   to be a mirror image (determinant -1), one axis is flipped;
4. `t = centre_to - R * centre_from`.

The error is the rms (typical size) of the distance left over. Result on our Go2: error 0.000 mm,
because the firmware used the same transform to make the copy.

That is why the next step exists: the mount comes from the firmware's own copy, so it is not an
independent measurement. The **floor** is.

*Floor.* `find_floor` uses **RANSAC**:

1. take 3 random points, make a plane through them;
2. keep it only if it is 0.2 to 0.7 m from the lidar (the floor, not a wall);
3. count the points within 3 cm of it;
4. repeat 600 times, keep the plane with most points;
5. refit that plane through all its points by least squares.

The random seed is fixed, so every run gives the same answer. At most 40000 points are used. Then
the floor normal is turned by `lidar_to_body`. If the mount is right it points straight up, and the
**floor tilt** is the angle to +z. Real value on our Go2: 0.82 deg (limit 2 deg).

*Range offset.* If every distance is too long by `o`, a flat floor looks like a bowl. A point whose
ray hits the floor at angle `a` from the vertical appears at `h + o * cos(a)` instead of `h`.
`fit_range_offset` fits `h` and `o` by least squares. Example from the formula: with `h` = 0.33 m
and `o` = 0.11 m, the point straight below reads 0.44 m deep, and a point at 60 deg reads
0.33 + 0.11 * 0.5 = 0.385 m. Real result on our Go2: `o` = 0.110 m.

#### 2. Body IMU, robot still

- `gyro_bias` = the mean of the still gyroscope. Its std (spread) is the noise.
- `accel_scale` = `9.81 / length of the mean accelerometer`. It is a **scale and not a bias**:
  standing still shows only the reading along gravity, and there a scale and a bias look the same.
  Real value: the still accelerometer reads 9.43 m/s^2, so `accel_scale` = 9.81 / 9.43 = 1.040.
- Body tilt = angle between the mean accelerometer and +z.

#### 3. Lidar IMU rotation (report only)

`fit_imu_rotation` matches the lidar IMU gyroscope to the body gyroscope with the same Kabsch fit,
but only for samples where the robot really turns (rate at least 0.15 rad/s). Both IMUs have a
different clock delay, so it tries every **lag** from -100 ms to +100 ms in 5 ms steps. For each lag
it pairs each sample with the nearest one (at most 3 ms away), fits, and keeps the lag with the
smallest error. A live run on 2026-10-19 found -40 ms.

The **axis spread** tells whether the robot turned about more than one axis. It is the second
largest over the largest eigenvalue of the rates' spread matrix. Spinning on the spot only turns about z,
and then the rotation about z cannot be told. The tilts add the other two axes.

#### 4. Lidar IMU bias and gravity

The still lidar gyroscope and accelerometer are rotated into the body frame with `imu_to_body`. The
mean gyroscope is the lidar IMU `gyro_bias`. The mean accelerometer against +z gives the gravity
tilt. It is synthetic on the Go2 (154 deg on ours), which is why the lidar IMU is not used.

#### 5. Leak

When the robot turns on the spot, a perfect gyroscope shows nothing on x and y. `fit_leak` measures
the share of the yaw rate that still shows there. Made-up example: yaw rate z = 1.0 rad/s and x reads
0.03, so `z_to_x` = -0.03. The correction would be `x += z_to_x * z`.

#### 6. Checks

Each check is one value against one limit. The defaults are what our Go2 gave on 2026-10-13, with a
wide margin.

| Check | Limit | Value on our Go2 |
|---|---|---|
| Body gyroscope noise | at most 0.02 rad/s | 0.007 |
| Body `accel_scale` error | at most 0.1 | 0.04 |
| Body gravity tilt | at most 5 deg | 1.2 to 2.2 |
| Matched lidar points | at least 1000 | 151577 |
| `lidar_to_body` error rms | at most 1 mm | 0.000 |
| Floor tilt after `lidar_to_body` | at most 2 deg | 0.82 |
| `imu_to_body` error rms | at most 0.25 rad/s | 0.12 |
| `imu_to_body` lag | at most 95 ms | |
| Turn axis spread | at least 0.01 | 0.04 |
| Still lidar gyroscope noise | at most 0.02 rad/s | 0.007 |
| `gyro_leak` left | at most 0.1 | 0.034 |

The lag limit is 95 ms and not 100: a lag on the edge of the search means the best lag is outside.

### The report and the file

`describe(calibration)` makes one line per result and one per check (`PASS` or `FAIL`). The terminal
and the comments at the top of the file show the same lines. `to_parameter_file` writes the YAML.
Numbers are always written with a decimal point and never as `1e-05`: the ROS parameter reader would
take `1` for an integer and `1e-05` for a string, and an array must not mix types.

## Types and files

.mermaid
    classDiagram
      class CalibrationBuffer
      class CalibrationData
      class Calibration
      class Limits
      class Check
      class RigidFit
      class Plane
      class RangeOffset
      class VectorStats
      class ImuRotationFit
      class RotationFit
      class GyroLeak
      CalibrationBuffer *-- CalibrationData : fills
      Calibration *-- RigidFit : lidar_to_body
      Calibration *-- Plane : floor
      Calibration *-- RangeOffset : range
      Calibration *-- VectorStats : gyro biases and accel
      Calibration *-- ImuRotationFit : imu_to_body
      Calibration *-- GyroLeak : leak
      Calibration *-- Check : checks
      ImuRotationFit *-- RotationFit : fit
      Limits ..> Check : sets the limits
      CalibrationData ..> Calibration : calibrate

| File | Does | ROS |
|---|---|---|
| `sequence` | Phases, the command at each moment, which phases are still or moving | no |
| `buffer` | `CalibrationBuffer`: samples sorted by phase, scans matched with their copy | no |
| `fitting` | Kabsch rotation fit (vectors) and rigid fit (points) | no |
| `floor` | RANSAC floor plane, tilt from +z, range offset | no |
| `imu_fit` | Pairing by time, IMU rotation with lag search, axis spread, statistics, leak | no |
| `calibrate` | The whole calibration and its checks | no |
| `report` | The report lines and the parameter file | no |
| `messages` | Topic names, message conversions, sport requests | yes |
| `bag_source` | `read_bag()`: a bag into the buffer | yes |
| `live_source` | `run_live()`: drives the robot and fills the buffer | yes |
| `main` | Settings, the linear flow, Ctrl-C | yes |

CMake builds the ROS-free files as `go2_calibration_core`, the others as `go2_calibration_ros`. The
executable and the tests link them. `messages.cpp` also turns a `Command` into the Unitree request
(Move and Euler carry `{"x":..,"y":..,"z":..}` as JSON, the API ids are 1002 BalanceStand, 1003
StopMove, 1007 Euler, 1008 Move).

## Parameters

All are ROS parameters (`-p name:=value` or `--params-file`). The defaults suit any Go2 with the L1.

| Parameter | Default | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `bag` | empty | Empty: live run. A bag folder: calibrate from it |
| `output` | `go2_calibration.yaml` | The file written |
| `node_name` | `go2_preprocessor` | The node the file is for (its top-level key) |
| `max_scans` | 100 | Matched scans kept for the lidar mount |
| `topics.cloud`, `topics.cloud_base`, `topics.imu` | `/utlidar/cloud`, `/utlidar/cloud_base`, `/utlidar/imu` | Lidar cloud, its body-frame copy, lidar IMU |
| `topics.body_state`, `topics.request`, `topics.phase` | `/sportmodestate`, `/api/sport/request`, `/calibration/phase` | Body IMU, sport commands, phase name |
| `motion.still_s`, `motion.pause_s` | 30, 3 | s. Range 10 to 300 and 1 to 30 |
| `motion.spin_rate`, `motion.spin_turns` | 1.0, 2 | rad/s (0.3 to 1.5), turns each way (0.5 to 5) |
| `motion.tilt_angle`, `motion.tilt_frequency`, `motion.tilt_s` | 0.2, 0.4, 15 | rad (0 to 0.3, 0 skips the tilts), Hz (0.1 to 1), s (2 to 60) |
| `limits.*` | the table in "Checks" | `min_matched_points`, `max_mount_error_mm`, `max_floor_tilt_deg`, `max_gyro_error`, `min_axis_spread`, `max_still_noise`, `max_leak`, `max_accel_scale_error`, `max_body_tilt_deg` |

A motion value outside its range stops the program before anything moves.

## Output

```yaml
# Go2 calibration for go2_preprocessor, written by go2_calibration on <date>
# from <bag or "a live run">.
# ... the report and the checks, as comments ...
go2_preprocessor:
  ros__parameters:
    lidar_to_body:
      rotation: [9 values, row-major]
      translation: [x, y, z]
    body_imu:
      gyro_bias: {x, y, z}
      accel_scale: <about 1.04>
    range_offset: <metres>
```

Convention: `p_body = R * p_sensor + t`, `R` row-major, body frame x forward, y left, z up. The body
IMU needs no rotation: `/sportmodestate` already uses the body axes.

.mermaid
    flowchart LR
      yaml[(go2_calibration.yaml)] -->|"lidar_to_body"| cl["cloud: lidar frame to body frame"]
      yaml -->|"body_imu"| im["IMU: remove bias, scale accelerometer"]
      yaml -.->|"range_offset"| ro["cloud: every lidar range shortened by it"]

## Build and test

The package needs Unitree's `unitree_api` and `unitree_go` messages. On the robot they come with
`~/unitree_ros2`:

```bash
cd ~/unitree_go2
source ~/unitree_ros2/setup.sh
colcon build --packages-select go2_calibration
colcon test --packages-select go2_calibration && colcon test-result --verbose
```

Laptop (foxy container), once: `sudo apt install ros-foxy-rosidl-generator-dds-idl`, clone
`https://github.com/unitreerobotics/unitree_ros2`, build only the messages
(`cd unitree_ros2/cyclonedds_ws && colcon build --packages-select unitree_api unitree_go`), and
source `cyclonedds_ws/install/local_setup.bash` after `/opt/ros/foxy/setup.bash`.

Tests cover every ROS-free file, the message conversions, and the bag source (on bags the tests
write). The live source drives a robot and has no unit test. It was checked on the laptop against
fake topics on an isolated domain (`ROS_LOCALHOST_ONLY=1 ROS_DOMAIN_ID=77`): no robot, a full run,
and Ctrl-C in the middle of a turn.

## Results on our Go2

Real numbers.

2026-10-13, bags `calib`, `standstill` and `spin` merged with phase messages (all checks PASS):

- `lidar_to_body` = R_y(165 deg) R_z(-123 deg), t = (0.282, 0, 0), error rms 0.000 mm.
- Floor tilt 0.82 deg. `range_offset` 0.110 m, lidar 0.330 m above the floor.
- `imu_to_body` is 0.65 deg from `lidar_to_body`: the lidar IMU gyroscope uses the cloud's axes.
- Lidar IMU `gyro_bias` (0.0028, -0.0055, 0.0006) rad/s. Gravity 154 deg from +z after `imu_to_body`.

2026-10-19, three live calibrations, body IMU:

- `accel_scale` 1.0385 to 1.0420. The still accelerometer reads 9.41 to 9.45 m/s^2.
- Gravity 1.2 to 2.2 deg from +z. Gyro bias about (-0.010, 0.011, -0.004) rad/s.
- Only `calibration_03` passed every check. The other two failed the floor tilt (2.05 and 2.83 deg),
  and `calibration_02` also the still noise.

## Known gaps

- **`range_offset` may depend on the distance.** `go2_preprocessor` subtracts one constant from every
  range. Over all floor points it is 0.110 m. Over the floor within 1.6 m only, an earlier analysis
  gave 0.128 m. Check it against a wall at taped distances.
- **The lidar mount is the firmware's value,** not an independent measurement. The floor tilt is the
  only independent check.
- **A bag needs `/calibration/phase`.** Old bags without it must be merged with phase messages first.
- **The lidar accelerometer is synthetic,** so the gravity direction of the lidar IMU is reported
  and never used.
